          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Four sentences for trying on clothes",
          "items": [
            "**אֶפְשָׁר לִמְדֹּד?** *(efshar limdod?)* — can I try it on?",
            "**יֵשׁ בְּמִדָּה יוֹתֵר גְּדוֹלָה?** *(yesh bemidah yoter gdolah?)* — do you have a bigger size?",
            "**יֵשׁ בְּצֶבַע אַחֵר?** *(yesh betseva akher?)* — do you have another color?",
            "**אֲנִי לוֹקֵחַ אֶת זֶה** *(ani loke'akh et zeh)* — I'll take this one. Note the אֶת: \"this one\" is definite."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "⚠️ **מִכְנָסַיִם** and **נַעֲלַיִם** end in ־ַיִם, the **pair** marker — a leftover special form for things that come in twos. Just like יָדַיִם (two hands), עֵינַיִם (two eyes), אָזְנַיִם (two ears). English says \"a pair of pants\"; Hebrew builds the pair into the word."
        }
